# -*- coding: utf-8 -*-
"""Original (paraphrased) constellation accounts for constellations.json.

Each entry: abbr -> (origin, year, story).  `year` is the year the figure entered the
western tradition in its current form (150 = Ptolemy's Almagest, c. AD 150).  The build
script (enrich-constellations.py) appends one computed sentence about the real 3D spread
of the figure's stars.
Sources consulted for facts: Wikipedia (CC BY-SA), Ian Ridpath's Star Tales, Ptolemy's
Almagest, Bayer's Uranometria, Hevelius' Prodromus Astronomiae, Lacaille's Coelum australe
stelliferum.  Text is original wording released under the repository licence.
"""

PTOL = "Ptolemy"
KD = "Keyser & de Houtman / Plancius 1598"
HEV = "Hevelius 1690"
LAC = "Lacaille 1756"
PLA = "Plancius 1612-13"

LORE = {
"Hya": (PTOL, 150, "Hydra is the largest of the 88 constellations, a long trail of faint stars winding south of Cancer, Leo and Virgo. In Greek tradition it is the many-headed water serpent that Heracles killed as his second labour, and the sky-snake carries the Cup (Crater) and the Crow (Corvus) on its back. Its one conspicuous star is Alphard, the orange 'solitary one'. The constellation also holds the open cluster M48, the globular cluster M68 and the barred spiral M83, the Southern Pinwheel."),
"Vir": (PTOL, 150, "Virgo is a zodiac constellation pictured as a maiden holding an ear of grain, which is the meaning of Spica, her brightest star; the Greeks linked her with Demeter, with the just maiden Astraea, or with Erigone. The Sun is in Virgo around the autumn equinox, and the figure is the second-largest constellation. Its northern part overlaps the Virgo Cluster, a swarm of galaxies including the giant elliptical M87 and the Sombrero Galaxy M104, and it contains 3C 273, the first quasar to have its redshift measured. Porrima (Gamma Virginis) is a close pair of sun-like stars."),
"UMa": (PTOL, 150, "Ursa Major is the Great Bear of Greek myth, usually identified with the nymph Callisto, whom Zeus placed among the stars after she was turned into a bear. Its seven brightest stars form the Plough or Big Dipper, an asterism known in Indian astronomy as the Saptarishi, the seven sages. The two end stars of the bowl, Dubhe and Merak, point toward Polaris, and Mizar and Alcor make a classic naked-eye double. The constellation also holds the galaxies M81 and M82, the Pinwheel M101 and the Owl Nebula M97. Five of the Dipper's stars (Beta to Zeta) belong to a common moving group, while Dubhe and Alkaid are drifting the other way."),
"Cet": (PTOL, 150, "Cetus is the sea monster sent by Poseidon to ravage the coast of Cepheus's kingdom after Cassiopeia boasted of her daughter's beauty; Perseus slew it. Its star Mira (Omicron Ceti) was the first periodic variable star recognised, noted by David Fabricius in 1596, and swings from naked-eye brightness to near invisibility over about eleven months. Diphda (Beta Ceti) is its brightest star, and nearby Tau Ceti, only about 3.6 parsecs away, is one of the closest sun-like stars. The spiral galaxy M77 lies within the figure."),
"Her": (PTOL, 150, "Hercules depicts the hero Heracles, whom Ptolemy knew only as 'the kneeling one' (Engonasin) without a firm identity. Four stars form the Keystone, which holds the Great Globular Cluster M13, one of the finest in the northern sky; M92 is also here. Rasalgethi (Alpha Herculis), 'head of the kneeler', is a red giant and double star. The Sun is moving relative to the nearby stars toward a point, the solar apex, close to the Hercules-Lyra border. The distant Hercules Cluster of galaxies lies in the same constellation."),
"Eri": (PTOL, 150, "Eridanus is a river that winds from near Rigel in Orion deep into the southern sky; the Greeks variously identified it with the Po, the Nile or the river into which Phaethon fell after losing control of the Sun's chariot. Its southern end is marked by Achernar, whose Arabic name means 'the end of the river'; it is one of the most flattened stars known because it spins so fast. Epsilon Eridani, a young sun-like star about 3.2 parsecs away, has a debris disc and a confirmed planet. Keid (40 Eridani) is a triple system that includes a white dwarf."),
"Peg": (PTOL, 150, "Pegasus is the winged horse that sprang from the blood of Medusa when Perseus killed her, and was later ridden by Bellerophon. The Great Square of Pegasus is a famous autumn sight; its north-eastern corner is Alpheratz, which now belongs to Andromeda but was once also called Delta Pegasi. The globular cluster M15 and the galaxy group Stephan's Quintet lie within the constellation. In 1995 the planet around 51 Pegasi, found by Michel Mayor and Didier Queloz, became the first discovered orbiting a sun-like star."),
"Dra": (PTOL, 150, "Draco is the dragon Ladon, who guarded the golden apples of the Hesperides until Heracles killed him, though some tellings make it a dragon hurled into the sky by Athena. It winds between the two Bears, and Thuban (Alpha Draconis) was the pole star around 2700 BC, in the age of the Egyptian pyramids. At Eltanin (Gamma Draconis) James Bradley, trying to measure parallax, discovered the aberration of starlight in 1725-28. The north pole of the ecliptic lies in Draco, as does the Cat's Eye Nebula, NGC 6543."),
"Cen": (PTOL, 150, "Centaurus is a centaur, often identified with the wise Chiron, tutor of heroes; Ptolemy's version also took in the stars that were later split off as Crux. Alpha Centauri, a triple system, holds the nearest star to the Sun, Proxima Centauri, at about 1.3 parsecs. Omega Centauri, listed by Ptolemy as a star and recognised as a nebulous object by Halley in 1677, is the largest globular cluster in the Milky Way. The radio galaxy Centaurus A, NGC 5128, is one of the closest active galaxies. Hadar (Beta Centauri) and Rigil Kentaurus (Alpha) are the two Pointers to the Southern Cross."),
"Aqr": (PTOL, 150, "Aquarius is the water-bearer, a figure pouring a stream of water that flows toward Piscis Austrinus; the Greeks linked him to Ganymede, cupbearer of the gods, or to Deucalion of the great flood, and the image goes back to Babylonian astronomy. Its brightest stars, Sadalsuud and Sadalmelik, have Arabic names meaning 'luck of lucks' and 'luck of the king'. The Helix Nebula, NGC 7293, is one of the nearest planetary nebulae, and the globular cluster M2 is also here. The red dwarf TRAPPIST-1, with its seven rocky planets, lies in Aquarius about 12 parsecs away. The Eta Aquariids meteor shower is debris from Halley's Comet."),
"Oph": (PTOL, 150, "Ophiuchus is the serpent-bearer, usually identified with Asclepius, the healer, who holds Serpens in his hands while standing on the Scorpion. Rasalhague, 'the head of the serpent collector', is its brightest star. Barnard's Star, a red dwarf about 1.8 parsecs away, has the largest proper motion of any known star, and Kepler's supernova of 1604 flared in this constellation. The Sun's path crosses the southern part of Ophiuchus from about 30 November to 17 December, though it is not one of the traditional twelve zodiac signs. The globular clusters M10 and M12 are visible in modest telescopes."),
"Leo": (PTOL, 150, "Leo is one of the oldest recognised constellations, a lion often identified with the Nemean lion killed by Heracles. The Sickle, a backward question mark of stars, marks its head and mane and ends at Regulus, 'the little king'. Algieba (Gamma Leonis) is a fine golden double star, and Denebola marks the tail. The Leonid meteor shower radiates from the Sickle each November. The Leo Triplet (M65, M66, NGC 3628) and the pair M95 and M96 are well-known galaxies, and the red dwarf Wolf 359 is one of the nearest stars to the Sun."),
"Boo": (PTOL, 150, "Boötes is the herdsman, drawn as a man driving the Bears around the pole; later he was identified with Arcas, son of Callisto, or with a ploughman who invented the plough. Arcturus, 'guardian of the bear', is the brightest star of the northern celestial hemisphere and the fourth brightest in the whole night sky, an orange giant about 11 parsecs away. The huge Boötes Void, a region with unusually few galaxies and hundreds of millions of light-years across, was found in 1981. The Quadrantid meteors appear to radiate from the border of Boötes, from the obsolete constellation Quadrans Muralis."),
"Psc": (PTOL, 150, "Pisces shows two fish tied together by cords, said in Greek myth to be Aphrodite and Eros, who escaped the monster Typhon by turning into fish and tying themselves together. It is a faint zodiac constellation whose brighter points include Alpherg (Eta Piscium) and the knot at Alrescha, 'the cord'. The vernal equinox, the zero point of right ascension, currently lies in Pisces because of the precession of the equinoxes. The face-on spiral galaxy M74 is a favourite target for amateur astronomers."),
"Sgr": (PTOL, 150, "Sagittarius is the archer, a centaur drawing a bow toward Scorpius; the Greeks sometimes identified him with the satyr Crotus or the centaur Chiron. Its brightest stars form the Teapot asterism, with Kaus Australis ('southern part of the bow') as the brightest. The Sun lies in this constellation at the December solstice, and the centre of the Milky Way, the radio source Sagittarius A*, lies in this direction. The region holds many showpieces: the Lagoon Nebula M8, the Trifid Nebula M20, the Omega Nebula M17 and the globular cluster M22."),
"Cyg": (PTOL, 150, "Cygnus is the swan, linked in Greek myth with Zeus disguised as a swan, with Orpheus, or with Cycnus, a friend of Phaethon turned into a swan. Its brightest stars form the Northern Cross, and Deneb at the tail is one corner of the Summer Triangle. Albireo, the head, is a celebrated double star with golden and blue components. Cygnus X-1 was the first strong black hole candidate, and 61 Cygni was the first star whose distance was measured, by Friedrich Bessel in 1838. The North America Nebula and the Veil Nebula (a supernova remnant) lie here."),
"Tau": (PTOL, 150, "Taurus is the bull, often identified with Zeus in disguise carrying Europa across the sea; only the front half of the bull is drawn. Aldebaran, the orange eye, is not part of the Hyades cluster that forms the V of the face; it lies in the foreground, at less than half the cluster's distance. The Pleiades (M45), known as Krittika in India and Subaru in Japan, sit on the bull's shoulder, and the Crab Nebula (M1) is the remnant of the supernova recorded by Chinese astronomers in AD 1054. Elnath marks the tip of one horn."),
"Cam": (PLA, 1612, "Camelopardalis was introduced by the Dutch cartographer Petrus Plancius, appearing on a celestial globe of 1612-13 and later in Jakob Bartsch's star charts. It fills a large, faint region of northern sky between Ursa Major and Cassiopeia, near the pole, and has no star brighter than about magnitude 4. Plancius said his giraffe represented the camel on which Rebekah rode to meet Isaac. Beta Camelopardalis, its brightest star, is a double, and Alpha Camelopardalis is a very luminous and very remote blue supergiant. The spiral galaxy IC 342, partly hidden behind the Milky Way, is one of the nearer large galaxies."),
"And": (PTOL, 150, "Andromeda is the princess chained to a rock as a sacrifice to the sea monster Cetus because her mother Cassiopeia had boasted of her beauty; Perseus rescued her and married her. Alpheratz, at her head, is shared with the Great Square of Pegasus. The Andromeda Galaxy, M31, visible to the naked eye in dark skies, lies about 2.5 million light-years away and is expected to merge with the Milky Way in several billion years. Almach (Gamma Andromedae) is a beautiful golden and blue double star."),
"Pup": (LAC, 1756, "Puppis is the poop deck, the stern of the great ship Argo Navis that carried Jason and the Argonauts in search of the Golden Fleece. Argo Navis, listed by Ptolemy, was broken up by Nicolas-Louis de Lacaille in 1756 into Carina (the keel), Puppis and Vela (the sails). Naos (Zeta Puppis), an extremely hot blue supergiant, is its brightest star, and the open clusters M46 and M47 lie close together on its northern border. Much of the constellation lies in the Milky Way and includes the Puppis A supernova remnant."),
"Aur": (PTOL, 150, "Auriga is the charioteer, identified in Greek myth with Erichthonius, said to have invented the four-horse chariot, or with Myrtilus. He carries a she-goat, Capella, with her kids, a small triangle of stars beside her. Capella is the sixth-brightest star in the night sky, a pair of yellow giants close together. The clusters M36, M37 and M38 are fine in binoculars, and the eclipsing binary Epsilon Aurigae has eclipses lasting nearly two years each, recurring about every 27 years, most recently in 2009-11. Elnath, once Gamma Aurigae, was transferred to Taurus."),
"Aql": (PTOL, 150, "Aquila is the eagle that carried the thunderbolts of Zeus, and in some stories fetched the boy Ganymede to Olympus. Altair, 'the flying eagle', is the 12th brightest star in the night sky and only about 5 parsecs away; it spins so quickly that it is visibly flattened. With Vega and Deneb it forms the Summer Triangle. In the East Asian Tanabata story, Altair is the cowherd Hikoboshi who meets the weaver Vega once a year across the Milky Way. The constellation lies in a rich Milky Way region and has produced several bright novae, including Nova Aquilae 1918."),
"Ser": (PTOL, 150, "Serpens is the snake held by Ophiuchus, and it is the only constellation split into two separate parts: Serpens Caput, the head, and Serpens Cauda, the tail, with Ophiuchus in between. Unukalhai, 'the serpent's neck', is the brightest star, in the head. The globular cluster M5 lies in the head, while the tail holds the Eagle Nebula, M16, whose 'Pillars of Creation' were photographed by the Hubble Space Telescope in 1995. Serpens Cauda lies in a rich portion of the Milky Way."),
"Per": (PTOL, 150, "Perseus is the hero who beheaded the Gorgon Medusa, rode Pegasus and rescued Andromeda from the sea monster. Algol, the 'demon star' (Beta Persei), marks the head of Medusa and is the prototype eclipsing binary; its regular dimming every 2.9 days was explained by John Goodricke in 1783. The Perseid meteor shower comes from Comet Swift-Tuttle, and the Double Cluster (h and chi Persei) is a striking pair of young star clusters visible to the naked eye. Mirfak, the brightest star, is the leading member of the Alpha Persei Cluster."),
"Cas": (PTOL, 150, "Cassiopeia is the vain queen of Ethiopia who angered the sea nymphs by boasting of her beauty; she is placed in the sky on her throne, circling the pole and sometimes upside down as punishment. Her five brightest stars form a distinctive W or M. Tycho Brahe's supernova of 1572 blazed in Cassiopeia, and Cassiopeia A is the brightest radio source beyond the Solar System, the remnant of another supernova. Schedar and Caph are its brightest stars, and the open clusters M52 and M103 are here."),
"Ori": (PTOL, 150, "Orion is the hunter of Greek myth, a giant killed by the Scorpion or by Artemis, and in the sky he sets as Scorpius rises on the other side of the heavens. In ancient Egypt the same stars were the god Sah, paired with Sopdet (Sirius). His shoulder Betelgeuse is a red supergiant and his foot Rigel a blue supergiant; the three stars of his Belt point to Sirius in one direction and Aldebaran in the other. Below the Belt, the Orion Nebula M42, about 1,300 light-years away, is the nearest large star-forming region to the Sun. The Horsehead Nebula lies near the left Belt star."),
"Cep": (PTOL, 150, "Cepheus is the king of Ethiopia, husband of Cassiopeia and father of Andromeda, depicted as a house-shaped figure. Delta Cephei, whose variability John Goodricke found in 1784, is the prototype of the Cepheid variables; Henrietta Leavitt's period-luminosity relation for such stars gave astronomers a way of measuring distances to other galaxies. Herschel's Garnet Star (Mu Cephei) is among the reddest naked-eye stars. Alderamin (Alpha Cephei) will lie near the north pole of the sky around AD 7500."),
"Lyn": (HEV, 1690, "Lynx is a faint constellation devised by Johannes Hevelius, who published it in his star atlas of 1690 and reportedly said one would need the eyes of a lynx to see it. It occupies a large stretch between Ursa Major and Auriga, with few stars brighter than magnitude 3.5. NGC 2419, nicknamed the Intergalactic Wanderer, is a remote globular cluster in the outer halo of the Milky Way, roughly 300,000 light-years away."),
"Lib": (PTOL, 150, "Libra is the scales, the only zodiac sign that represents an object rather than a creature. Before the Romans made it a constellation of its own, its stars were thought of as the claws of Scorpius, and the names of Zubenelgenubi and Zubeneschamali, 'southern claw' and 'northern claw', still reflect this. The Gliese 581 system, with several planets found by radial-velocity surveys, lies about 6 parsecs away in Libra. The Sun passes through the constellation from late October to late November."),
"Gem": (PTOL, 150, "Gemini depicts the twins Castor and Pollux, sons of Leda, one mortal and one immortal in Greek myth, who were set in the sky by Zeus; earlier Mesopotamian astronomy knew the same stars as a pair of twin gods. Castor is a system of six stars and Pollux, the brighter, hosts a giant planet. Uranus was discovered here by William Herschel in 1781, and Pluto by Clyde Tombaugh in 1930. The Geminid meteors, the strongest annual shower, come from the asteroid 3200 Phaethon. M35 is a bright open cluster."),
"Cnc": (PTOL, 150, "Cancer is the crab that Hera sent to bite Heracles while he fought the Hydra; the hero crushed it and Hera placed it in the sky. It is the faintest of the zodiac constellations. At its centre lies the Beehive Cluster, Praesepe or M44, visible to the naked eye as a hazy patch, flanked by the two Aselli, 'donkey colts', Asellus Borealis and Australis. 55 Cancri, also known as Copernicus, was the first star found to have five planets. The old open cluster M67 lies near the southern border."),
"Vel": (LAC, 1756, "Vela is the sail of Argo Navis, the ship of Jason, the old Ptolemaic constellation divided by Lacaille in 1756 into Vela, Carina and Puppis. Gamma Velorum, a bright Wolf-Rayet and O-star binary, is its brightest star. The Vela supernova remnant, about 11,000 years old, contains the Vela Pulsar, one of the first and best-studied pulsars. Delta and Kappa Velorum, with Epsilon and Iota Carinae, form the False Cross, often mistaken for the Southern Cross."),
"Sco": (PTOL, 150, "Scorpius is the scorpion that, in Greek myth, killed Orion; the two are kept on opposite sides of the sky. Antares, 'the rival of Ares' (Mars), is a red supergiant at its heart. The curved tail ends in the stinger Shaula and Lesath, and in Polynesian tradition it forms the fishhook of Maui. The constellation lies in a bright part of the Milky Way with the open clusters M6 and M7 and the globular cluster M4, and most of its stars belong to the nearby Scorpius-Centaurus OB association."),
"Car": (LAC, 1756, "Carina is the keel of Argo Navis, the ship of Jason, carved out of Ptolemy's Argo by Lacaille in 1756. Canopus, named after the helmsman of Menelaus, is the second-brightest star in the night sky. The great Carina Nebula, NGC 3372, contains Eta Carinae, a massive star that erupted in the 1840s to become the second-brightest star in the sky for a time, and the Keyhole Nebula. The Southern Pleiades, IC 2602, and the Diamond Cross asterism lie within the constellation. Miaplacidus and Avior are its other bright stars."),
"Mon": (PLA, 1612, "Monoceros was introduced by Petrus Plancius, appearing on his globe of about 1612, and was taken up by Jakob Bartsch in 1624. It fills a faint region between Orion and Canis Major along the winter Milky Way. The Rosette Nebula with its central cluster NGC 2244, the Christmas Tree Cluster with the Cone Nebula, and the open cluster M50 are good targets. Beta Monocerotis is a striking triple star that William Herschel described in 1781 as among the most beautiful sights in the heavens."),
"Scl": (LAC, 1756, "Sculptor was introduced by Nicolas-Louis de Lacaille from his observations at the Cape of Good Hope in 1751-52, originally as the Sculptor's Studio ('l'Atelier du Sculpteur'). It is a faint constellation, with Alpha Sculptoris only about magnitude 4.3. The south galactic pole lies within it, so it offers a clear view out of the Milky Way. The bright spiral NGC 253, the Sculptor Galaxy, and the dwarf spheroidal galaxy Sculptor Dwarf, a satellite of the Milky Way, are here."),
"Phe": (KD, 1598, "Phoenix was devised from observations made by the Dutch navigators Pieter Dirkszoon Keyser and Frederick de Houtman on their voyage to the East Indies in 1595-97, and it was put on the globe of Petrus Plancius in 1598 and in Bayer's atlas of 1603. It represents the mythical bird that is reborn from its own ashes. Ankaa, Alpha Phoenicis, is the brightest star, from an Arabic word for the phoenix. The Phoenix Dwarf is a distant dwarf galaxy at the edge of the Local Group, and the meteor shower called the Phoenicids was strong in 1956."),
"CVn": (HEV, 1690, "Canes Venatici, the hunting dogs, was introduced by Johannes Hevelius in his atlas of 1690, showing two dogs, Asterion and Chara, held on a leash by the herdsman Boötes. Cor Caroli, the brightest star, was named in the 17th century for a King Charles of England (accounts differ on whether this meant Charles I or Charles II) and is a fine double. Its galaxies are more famous than its stars: the Whirlpool Galaxy M51, M63 the Sunflower, M94 and M106, as well as the globular cluster M3."),
"Ari": (PTOL, 150, "Aries is the ram of the Golden Fleece, which Jason and the Argonauts set out to retrieve, in Greek myth. Hamal, its brightest star, comes from an Arabic word for 'lamb'. Some 2,000 years ago the vernal equinox lay in Aries, so the equinox is still called the First Point of Aries, although precession has since carried it into Pisces. The figure is a modest line of stars, with Sheratan and Mesarthim forming the horns."),
"Cap": (PTOL, 150, "Capricornus is the sea-goat, a creature with the front of a goat and the tail of a fish; its image goes back to Babylonian astronomy, where it represented the god Ea. In Greek myth it was associated with the satyr Pan, who plunged into a river and was transformed with a fish's tail. The Sun was at the December solstice in this constellation around 2,000 years ago, which gave the Tropic of Capricorn its name. Alpha Capricorni is a naked-eye pair of stars that are unrelated, one much farther than the other, and Deneb Algedi is the brightest star. M30 is a globular cluster."),
"For": (LAC, 1756, "Fornax was introduced by Lacaille in 1756 as the Chemical Furnace ('le Fourneau'), one of his tributes to science and the arts, devised from his observations at the Cape of Good Hope. It is a faint constellation with Alpha Fornacis, also called Dalim, its brightest star. It holds the Fornax Cluster of galaxies and the Fornax Dwarf, a satellite of the Milky Way, as well as the radio galaxy Fornax A. The Hubble Ultra Deep Field of 2004 looked at a tiny patch of Fornax."),
"Com": (PTOL, 150, "Coma Berenices commemorates the hair of Queen Berenice II of Egypt, who offered it to the gods in thanks for the safe return of her husband Ptolemy III from war; the court astronomer Conon of Samos declared that the vanished lock had been placed in the sky, a story told in a poem of Callimachus. Ptolemy treated the stars as part of Leo, and the figure became a constellation in its own right in the 16th century. It is dominated by the Coma Star Cluster (Melotte 111), a loose group visible to the naked eye. The north galactic pole lies here, giving a clear view to distant galaxies, including M64, the Black Eye Galaxy, M85 and M100."),
"CMa": (PTOL, 150, "Canis Major is the larger of Orion's two hunting dogs, or the dog Laelaps that never lost its prey. Sirius, the Dog Star, is the brightest star in the night sky, only 2.6 parsecs away, with a white dwarf companion; in ancient Egypt its dawn rising (as Sopdet) heralded the Nile flood, and in Roman usage the 'dog days' of summer were named for it. Mirzam, Adhara, Wezen and Aludra are also bright, and the open cluster M41 is nearby. The Canis Major Dwarf galaxy, found in 2003, was the nearest galaxy known at the time."),
"Pav": (KD, 1598, "Pavo, the peacock, was devised from Keyser and de Houtman's southern observations of 1595-97, mapped by Petrus Plancius in 1598 and shown in Bayer's 1603 Uranometria. The peacock was the bird of the goddess Hera in Greek tradition. The brightest star is Alpha Pavonis, simply called Peacock. NGC 6752, one of the brightest globular clusters, shines here, along with the spiral galaxy NGC 6744."),
"Gru": (KD, 1598, "Grus, the crane, was introduced from Keyser and de Houtman's observations by Petrus Plancius and then published by Bayer in 1603. Some earlier sources had treated its stars as part of Piscis Austrinus. Alnair, 'the bright one', is the brightest star. The constellation includes the Grus Quartet, four interacting galaxies."),
"Lup": (PTOL, 150, "Lupus, the wolf, was described by Ptolemy as an unnamed beast (Therion) held by the neighbouring Centaur as an offering at an altar, Ara; the Romans later called it a wolf. It lies in a bright part of the Milky Way. In AD 1006 a supernova, the brightest recorded stellar event in history, was seen here; its remnant, SN 1006, is still studied by astronomers. The dark cloud Lupus 3 is a nearby star-forming region about 150 parsecs away. Alpha Lupi and Beta Lupi are blue giants in the Scorpius-Centaurus association."),
"Sex": (HEV, 1690, "Sextans, the sextant, was introduced by Johannes Hevelius in 1687 and published in his Firmamentum Sobiescianum in 1690, commemorating the astronomical sextant he used for measuring star positions, which was destroyed in the fire that consumed his observatory in 1679. It is a faint constellation along the celestial equator south of Leo. Alpha Sextantis is its brightest star at around magnitude 4.5. The Spindle Galaxy (NGC 3115) is the best-known object in it."),
"Tuc": (KD, 1598, "Tucana, the toucan, was devised from the southern observations of Keyser and de Houtman and appears on Plancius's globe of 1598 and in Bayer's Uranometria. It contains the Small Magellanic Cloud, the satellite galaxy about 200,000 light-years away, and the globular cluster 47 Tucanae, the second brightest in the sky after Omega Centauri. Alpha Tucanae is its brightest star, an orange giant."),
"Ind": (KD, 1598, "Indus, the Indian, was introduced from Keyser and de Houtman's observations of the 1590s and published by Bayer in 1603; the figure represents a native inhabitant of the East Indies or America, shown in the atlas with arrows. Epsilon Indi is a nearby star, only about 3.6 parsecs away, with two brown dwarf companions and a giant planet. The constellation also holds the galaxy NGC 7049. Alpha Indi is its brightest star."),
"Oct": (LAC, 1756, "Octans was introduced by Nicolas-Louis de Lacaille in 1756 to honour the octant, the reflecting instrument John Hadley invented in 1730, which was used for navigation. Octans lies over the south celestial pole, so it holds the pole star of the southern hemisphere, Sigma Octantis, a very faint star of about magnitude 5.4 that is not a convenient pointer. Nu Octantis is its brightest star."),
"Lep": (PTOL, 150, "Lepus, the hare, sits beneath the feet of Orion, hunted by his dogs Canis Major and Canis Minor. Arneb, 'the hare', is its brightest star, a yellow supergiant, and Nihal marks the body. R Leporis, called Hind's Crimson Star, is a vivid red carbon star discovered by John Russell Hind in 1845. The globular cluster M79 lies within the constellation."),
"Lyr": (PTOL, 150, "Lyra is the lyre of Orpheus, made by Hermes from a tortoise shell and placed in the sky after the death of Orpheus. Vega is the fifth-brightest star in the night sky and a corner of the Summer Triangle; it was the pole star around 12,000 BC and will be again about AD 13,700. In 1850 it became the first star other than the Sun to be photographed, and it is the Weaver Girl Orihime of the Japanese Tanabata festival. The Ring Nebula M57 lies between Beta and Gamma Lyrae, and Epsilon Lyrae is a famous double-double."),
"Crt": (PTOL, 150, "Crater is the cup of Apollo, set on the back of Hydra beside Corvus the crow, linked to a myth in which the crow was sent to fetch water but dallied, then blamed the water serpent and was condemned to thirst. It is a faint constellation, with Delta Crateris at magnitude 3.6, and holds no Messier objects. The galaxy NGC 3981 is a notable spiral."),
"Col": ("Plancius 1592", 1592, "Columba, the dove, was introduced by Petrus Plancius in 1592, carved from stars that Ptolemy had counted in Canis Major, and was published in Bayer's Uranometria of 1603. Plancius is said to have associated it with the dove that Noah sent from the ark, while others link it with the Argonauts' dove. Its brightest star is Phact, from an Arabic word for a ring-dove. The constellation lies south of Lepus and Canis Major."),
"Vul": (HEV, 1690, "Vulpecula, the little fox, was first devised by Johannes Hevelius as Vulpecula cum Ansere, 'the little fox with the goose', in his 1690 atlas, but the goose was later dropped. It lies in the Milky Way between Cygnus and Aquila. The Dumbbell Nebula M27 was the first planetary nebula found, in 1764 by Charles Messier, and the open cluster Brocchi's Cluster, or Coathanger, is a binocular favourite. The first pulsar, PSR B1919+21, was discovered here in 1967 by Jocelyn Bell Burnell."),
"UMi": (PTOL, 150, "Ursa Minor is the Little Bear, in Greek myth Arcas or another bear set beside his mother Callisto. It contains Polaris, the current pole star, which lies less than a degree from the north celestial pole; the constellation forms the Little Dipper with Polaris at the end of its handle. Kochab and Pherkad, the Guardians of the Pole, are bright stars in the bowl. Polaris is a Cepheid variable and a triple system. The Ursa Minor Dwarf galaxy, a satellite of the Milky Way, was found in 1954."),
"Tel": (LAC, 1756, "Telescopium was introduced by Lacaille in 1756, commemorating the telescope, the instrument that transformed astronomy. It lies in a sparse part of the southern sky south of Sagittarius and Corona Australis. Alpha Telescopii is its brightest star at about magnitude 3.5, and RR Telescopii is a symbiotic star that flared as a nova in 1944."),
"Hor": (LAC, 1756, "Horologium, the pendulum clock, was introduced by Lacaille in 1756 to honour Christiaan Huygens, who invented the pendulum clock in 1656. It is a long, faint chain of stars in the southern sky, with Alpha Horologii, an orange giant, the brightest at a modest magnitude of 3.9. The Horologium-Reticulum Supercluster is a vast structure of galaxy clusters that lies in this direction."),
"Pic": (LAC, 1756, "Pictor was introduced by Lacaille in 1756 as Equuleus Pictoris, the painter's easel, one of his tributes to the arts and sciences, and the shorter name was adopted later. Alpha Pictoris is its brightest star. Beta Pictoris, about 19 parsecs away, is a young star with a famous dusty debris disc and a giant planet imaged directly. Kapteyn's Star, one of the nearest known halo stars, lies in Pictor, and the radio galaxy Pictor A is also here."),
"PsA": (PTOL, 150, "Piscis Austrinus, the southern fish, is the fish drinking the water poured by Aquarius, and was sometimes regarded in Greek tradition as the parent of the two fish of Pisces. Fomalhaut, 'the mouth of the fish', is one of the brightest stars, a young star about 7.7 parsecs away with a dusty ring that was among the first stars with an imaged planet candidate. It is one of the four Royal Stars of ancient Persia."),
"Hyi": (KD, 1598, "Hydrus, the male water snake, was devised from Keyser and de Houtman's observations in the 1590s and published by Bayer in 1603. It is not to be confused with Hydra, the large female serpent; the name Hydrus means 'water snake' in Latin. It winds between Achernar and the Small Magellanic Cloud near the south celestial pole. Beta Hydri, a yellow subgiant about 7 parsecs away, is the brightest star and is often described as a preview of the Sun's future."),
"Ant": (LAC, 1756, "Antlia, the air pump, was introduced by Lacaille in 1756 as Antlia Pneumatica, honouring the vacuum pump developed in the 17th century by Denis Papin and Robert Boyle. It is a faint constellation south of Hydra, with Alpha Antliae, an orange giant, its brightest star at about magnitude 4.3. The Antlia Dwarf galaxy and the spiral NGC 2997 are among its objects."),
"Ara": (PTOL, 150, "Ara, the altar, is the altar on which the gods swore an oath of alliance before fighting the Titans, as told in Greek myth. It lies south of Scorpius. The globular cluster NGC 6397 is among the nearest to the Earth, and Westerlund 1, discovered in 1961, is one of the most massive young star clusters known in the Milky Way."),
"LMi": (HEV, 1690, "Leo Minor, the little lion, was introduced by Johannes Hevelius in his atlas of 1690, between Leo and Ursa Major. It is a faint constellation with no stars brighter than about magnitude 3.8; 46 Leonis Minoris, called Praecipua, is the brightest. Hanny's Voorwerp, a green cloud of gas, was found here in 2007 by Hanny van Arkel, a Dutch schoolteacher taking part in the Galaxy Zoo project."),
"Pyx": (LAC, 1756, "Pyxis, the mariner's compass, was introduced by Lacaille in 1756 as Pyxis Nautica, a tribute to the navigator's compass, and it lies in the area of the old Argo Navis near Puppis. It is a faint constellation with Alpha Pyxidis, a hot blue giant, as brightest star at magnitude 3.7. T Pyxidis is a recurrent nova, with eruptions recorded in 1890, 1902, 1920, 1944, 1967 and 2011."),
"Mic": (LAC, 1756, "Microscopium, the microscope, was introduced by Lacaille in 1756 as one of his instrument constellations, a tribute to the compound microscope. It is a very faint constellation between Sagittarius and Piscis Austrinus, with no star brighter than about magnitude 4.7. Gamma Microscopii, a yellow giant, is the brightest."),
"Aps": (KD, 1598, "Apus, the bird of paradise, was devised from Keyser and de Houtman's observations and appeared on a globe by Plancius in 1598 and in Bayer's atlas of 1603. The Latin name derives from a Greek word meaning 'without feet', because the skins of birds of paradise brought to Europe had been prepared without legs, and the birds were believed to have none. It lies near the south celestial pole, and Alpha Apodis is its brightest star, an orange giant."),
"Lac": (HEV, 1690, "Lacerta, the lizard, was introduced by Johannes Hevelius in his atlas of 1690, between Cygnus, Cassiopeia and Andromeda; he also called it Stellio. The figure is a zigzag of faint stars that sometimes resembles a small W. It lies in the Milky Way. BL Lacertae, long classed as a variable star, was the prototype of the BL Lac objects, blazars whose jets point at Earth."),
"Del": (PTOL, 150, "Delphinus, the dolphin, is associated with the dolphin that carried the musician Arion to safety, or the one that found Amphitrite for Poseidon. Its small diamond of stars is known as Job's Coffin. The names of its two brightest stars, Sualocin and Rotanev, first appeared in the Palermo star catalogue of 1814 and are the name of Piazzi's assistant Niccolo Cacciatore in Latin, Nicolaus Venator, spelled backwards. The globular cluster NGC 6934 and the double star Gamma Delphini are within it."),
"Crv": (PTOL, 150, "Corvus, the crow, was Apollo's messenger, sent to fetch water in a cup (Crater) but delayed by a fig tree until the fruit ripened; he blamed the water serpent Hydra and was set in the sky beside it. The four brightest stars form a distinctive quadrilateral, and Gienah (Gamma Corvi) is the brightest. The interacting Antennae Galaxies, NGC 4038 and 4039, lie within it."),
"CMi": (PTOL, 150, "Canis Minor, the lesser dog, is the smaller of Orion's two hunting dogs, or in some versions the dog Maera that belonged to Icarius. Procyon means 'before the dog', because it rises just ahead of Sirius; it is the eighth-brightest star in the night sky, only about 3.5 parsecs away, and has a white dwarf companion. Gomeisa (Beta Canis Minoris) is the other bright star. With Betelgeuse and Sirius, Procyon forms the Winter Triangle."),
"Dor": (KD, 1598, "Dorado, the dolphinfish or swordfish, was devised from Keyser and de Houtman's observations in the 1590s and was published on Plancius's globe in 1598 and in Bayer's atlas. Most of the Large Magellanic Cloud lies within Dorado, including the Tarantula Nebula, the most active starburst region of the Local Group, and the supernova SN 1987A. The south ecliptic pole is also here. Alpha Doradus is the brightest star, and Beta Doradus is a bright Cepheid variable."),
"CrB": (PTOL, 150, "Corona Borealis, the northern crown, is a neat semicircle of stars identified in Greek myth with the crown of Ariadne, given by Dionysus when he married her after she was abandoned by Theseus. Alphecca (Alpha Coronae Borealis), also called Gemma, is the brightest star. T Coronae Borealis, the Blaze Star, is a recurrent nova that erupted in 1866 and 1946, and another outburst has long been anticipated. The Corona Borealis Supercluster is a collection of remote galaxy clusters."),
"Nor": (LAC, 1756, "Norma, the carpenter's level, was introduced by Lacaille in 1756 as Norma et Regula, 'the square and rule', one of the tools of the craftsman. It lies in a rich part of the Milky Way between Scorpius and Lupus. Gamma Normae is a naked-eye double, and the Norma Cluster of galaxies lies near the direction of the Great Attractor, a vast concentration of mass affecting the motion of galaxies in our region of space."),
"Men": (LAC, 1756, "Mensa, the Table Mountain, was introduced by Lacaille in 1756 as Mons Mensae, named after the mountain overlooking Cape Town, where he observed the southern sky; it is the only constellation named after a geographic feature on Earth. It is also one of the faintest constellations, with its brightest star, Alpha Mensae, at magnitude 5.1. Part of the Large Magellanic Cloud extends into it, and the south celestial pole is nearby."),
"Vol": (KD, 1598, "Volans, the flying fish, was devised from Keyser and de Houtman's observations of 1595-97 and published as Piscis Volans in Bayer's Uranometria of 1603. The fish is shown leaping from the sea, as flying fish were seen from Dutch ships in the tropics. It lies between Carina and the south pole, with Gamma Volantis, a fine double star, and Beta Volantis among its bright stars. The spiral galaxy NGC 2442 lies here."),
"Mus": (KD, 1598, "Musca, the fly, was devised from Keyser and de Houtman's observations of 1595-97 and was named Apis, the bee, in Bayer's Uranometria of 1603; it was later renamed as a fly. It lies next to Crux and the Coalsack, near the south celestial pole. Alpha Muscae is a hot blue giant, and the globular cluster NGC 4372 is a notable object."),
"Tri": (PTOL, 150, "Triangulum, the triangle, was known to the Greeks as Deltoton, because its shape resembles the Greek letter delta, and was associated with the delta of the Nile or with the island of Sicily, a triangular land. It lies between Andromeda and Aries. The Triangulum Galaxy, M33, is the third-largest member of the Local Group and about 2.7 million light-years away, and it can be seen with the naked eye under dark skies. Mothallah (Alpha Trianguli) is a double star."),
"Cha": (KD, 1598, "Chamaeleon, the chameleon, was devised from Keyser and de Houtman's observations of 1595-97 and published by Bayer in 1603. It lies close to the south celestial pole, in a region of dark clouds known for nearby star formation, at roughly 150-190 parsecs. The Eta Chamaeleontis cluster is a small, young group. Alpha Chamaeleontis is its brightest star."),
"CrA": (PTOL, 150, "Corona Australis, the southern crown, was listed by Ptolemy as a wreath of stars south of Sagittarius, and the Greeks thought of it as a crown or wreath. It is a small arc of stars next to the Milky Way. The nearby Corona Australis molecular cloud is a star-forming region about 130 parsecs away, and R Coronae Australis is a young variable star in a reflection nebula. Meridiana, Alpha Coronae Australis, is the brightest star."),
"Cae": (LAC, 1756, "Caelum, the chisel, was introduced by Lacaille in 1756 as Caela Sculptoris, the sculptor's chisel, a tribute to the arts. It is one of the faintest and least conspicuous constellations, with Alpha Caeli, its brightest star, at about magnitude 4.45. Lacaille mapped it from the Cape of Good Hope in 1751-52."),
"Ret": (LAC, 1756, "Reticulum, the reticle, was introduced by Lacaille in 1756 to honour the reticle, a grid of fine lines in an eyepiece that he used to measure star positions during his southern observations. It is a small constellation near the Large Magellanic Cloud, shaped like a diamond. Zeta Reticuli is a wide binary of sun-like stars about 12 parsecs away, and the spiral galaxy NGC 1313 is here."),
"TrA": (KD, 1598, "Triangulum Australe, the southern triangle, was devised from Keyser and de Houtman's observations in the 1590s and appeared in Bayer's Uranometria of 1603. It is a bright compact triangle lying in the Milky Way next to Circinus and Norma. Atria, a contraction of Alpha Trianguli Australis, is an orange giant and its brightest star. The open cluster NGC 6025 is a binocular object."),
"Sct": (HEV, 1690, "Scutum, the shield, was introduced by Johannes Hevelius, who in 1684 named it Scutum Sobiescianum, the Shield of Sobieski, in honour of John III Sobieski, King of Poland, whose forces broke the Ottoman siege of Vienna in 1683. It lies within the Milky Way and includes the Wild Duck Cluster, M11, one of the richest open clusters known. Delta Scuti is the prototype of the Delta Scuti pulsating variables, and the red supergiant UY Scuti is one of the largest known stars by radius."),
"Cir": (LAC, 1756, "Circinus, the compasses (a drawing instrument), was introduced by Lacaille in 1756 during his stay at the Cape of Good Hope. It is a small constellation along the Milky Way next to Alpha Centauri. Alpha Circini is its brightest star, a rapidly oscillating peculiar star. The Circinus Galaxy, an active spiral galaxy seen through the Milky Way's obscuring dust, was found in 1977, and Circinus X-1 is a bright X-ray binary."),
"Sge": (PTOL, 150, "Sagitta, the arrow, is one of the smallest constellations, an arrow-shaped figure between Aquila and Cygnus. It was identified by the Greeks as the arrow that Heracles used to kill the eagle devouring Prometheus' liver, or the arrow of Apollo or Cupid. The globular cluster M71 lies in it, and Gamma Sagittae is its brightest star, an orange giant. It has been recognised since antiquity and was listed by Ptolemy."),
"Equ": (PTOL, 150, "Equuleus, the pony or foal, is the second-smallest constellation. It was first catalogued by Hipparchus and later Ptolemy, and is usually identified with Celeris, a horse given to Castor by Hermes, or with a sibling of Pegasus. Kitalpha, Alpha Equulei, comes from an Arabic phrase meaning 'the section of the horse', and it is a yellow giant. The constellation lies between Delphinus and Pegasus."),
"Cru": ("Plancius 1589", 1589, "Crux, the Southern Cross, is the smallest of the 88 constellations. Its stars were counted in Centaurus in Ptolemy's time; European navigators such as Andrea Corsali described the Cross in the early 1500s, and Petrus Plancius made it a separate constellation on his celestial globe. Its brightest stars are Acrux, Mimosa, Gacrux and Imai; Acrux is a multiple star system, and Gacrux is a red giant. The Coalsack, a dark nebula, lies beside it, and the Jewel Box cluster NGC 4755 near Mimosa is a colourful open cluster. The Southern Cross appears on the flags of several southern-hemisphere nations."),
}
